@schema x.code/v1 { version: 1, relations: [x.code/touches, x.code/exercises] }

@decision d/g221f362dbc1d-1 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@221f362dbc1d }, "code:kind": act, "ingest:quote": sleep 630     # see the rate limit below: a new crate every 10 minutes after the first five }
~ Added a sleep of 630 seconds after the fifth crate to comply with crates.io rate limits.

To avoid hitting the rate limit and ensure all crates are published successfully.

@decision d/g221f362dbc1d-2 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@221f362dbc1d }, "code:kind": act, "ingest:quote": Check what landed before resuming: }
~ Included a command to check which crates have already landed before resuming publishing.

To handle cases where the publishing process is interrupted due to rate limits.

@decision d/g221f362dbc1d-3 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@221f362dbc1d }, "code:kind": act, "ingest:quote": sleep 630     # see the rate limit below: a new crate every 10 minutes after the first five }
~ Updated the sleep duration from 30 seconds to 630 seconds after the fifth crate.

To comply with crates.io's rate limit of one new crate per ten minutes after the initial burst of five.

@decision d/g221f362dbc1d-4 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@221f362dbc1d }, "code:kind": act, "ingest:quote": "**crates.io rate-limits new crates, and this workspace is eight of them.** The allowance is a burst of five, then one new crate per ten minutes." }
~ Documented the crates.io rate limits and how to handle them during publishing.

To provide guidance for future releases on how to manage the rate limits effectively.

@decision d/g221f362dbc1d-5 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@221f362dbc1d }, "code:kind": act, "ingest:quote": Check what landed before resuming: }
~ Added a command to check which crates have already landed before resuming publishing.

To handle cases where the publishing process is interrupted due to rate limits.

